(L_{3}\\) non‑sunny.\n\nBy Lemma 2 each of \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) contains at most one point of \\(S\\); therefore\ntogether they cover at most two points of \\(S\\).  \nConsequently at least \\(|S|-2 = 4\\) points of \\(S\\) must lie on the non‑sunny line\n\\(L_{3}\\).\n\nBut Lemma 3 states that a non‑sunny line can contain at most three points of\n\\(S\\). This contradiction shows that such a configuration cannot exist.\n\nHence for \\(n=3\\) there is **no** arrangement of three distinct lines with exactly\ntwo sunny lines covering all required points. ∎"
