            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le 4\\}\n   =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and \\(-1\\).\nHence a non‑sunny line must be parallel to one of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_v:\\;x=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_d:\\;x+y=4 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nEach line in (1) contains exactly three points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_h\\cap S &=\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\},\\\\\n\\ell_v\\cap S &=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\},\\\\\n\\ell_d\\cap S &=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nNo other point of \\(S\\) lies on any of these lines, so each intersection has size three. ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is not parallel to any line in (1) (i.e. \\(L\\) is sunny), then \\(L\\) contains **at most two** points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(S\\). Any three non‑collinear points determine a unique plane, but here we are in the plane, so the three points would be collinear. Compute the slopes of all possible pairs of points of \\(S\\); the only slopes that occur are \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and \\(-1\\). Consequently any line passing through two points of \\(S\\) is parallel to one of the lines in (1). Hence a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) cannot pass through three points of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 3  \nFix a non‑sunny line \\(L_0\\) from the set \\(\\{\\ell_h,\\ell_v,\\ell_d\\}\\).  \nLet \\(T=S\\setminus L_0\\) be the three points of \\(S\\) not lying on \\(L_0\\).  \nAny two distinct points of \\(T\\) are aligned with a line parallel to one of the *other* two lines in (1).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe treat the three possibilities for \\(L_0\\).\n\n*If \\(L_0=\\ell_h\\):* then \\(L_0\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) and  \n\\(T=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nPairs in \\(T\\):\n\\[\n(1,2)-(1,3)\\text{ vertical }(\\parallel\\ell_v),\\qquad\n(1,2)-(2,2)\\text{ horizontal }(\\parallel\\ell_h),\\qquad\n(1,3)-(2,2)\\text{ slope }-1\\;(\\parallel\\ell_d).\n\\]\n\n*If \\(L_0=\\ell_v\\):* then \\(L_0\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) and  \n\\(T=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nPairs in \\(T\\):\n\\[\n(2,1)-(2,2)\\text{ vertical }(\\parallel\\ell_v),\\qquad\n(2,1)-(3,1)\\text{ horizontal }(\\parallel\\ell_h),\\qquad\n(2,2)-(3,1)\\text{ slope }-1\\;(\\parallel\\ell_d).\n\\]\n\n*If \\(L_0=\\ell_d\\):* then \\(L_0\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) and  \n\\(T=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nPairs in \\(T\\):\n\\[\n(1,1)-(1,2)\\text{ vertical }(\\parallel\\ell_v),\\qquad\n(1,1)-(2,1)\\text{ horizontal }(\\parallel\\ell_h),\\qquad\n(1,2)-(2,1)\\text{ slope }-1\\;(\\parallel\\ell_d).\n\\]\n\nThus in every case each pair of points of \\(T\\) lies on a line parallel to one of the *other* two forbidden directions. ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 4  \nA sunny line can contain **at most one** point of the set \\(T\\) defined in Lemma 3.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a sunny line contained two distinct points of \\(T\\), those two points would be collinear with a line parallel to one of the forbidden directions by Lemma 3, contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility for \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exist three distinct lines\n\\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) satisfying the problem conditions with exactly two of them sunny.\nWithout loss of generality let \\(L_1\\) be the (unique) non‑sunny line and\n\\(L_2,L_3\\) be sunny.\n\nBy Lemma 1, \\(L_1\\) must be one of the three lines in (1) and therefore contains\nexactly three points of \\(S\\).  \nLet \\(T=S\\setminus L_1\\); then \\(|T|=3\\).\n\nBy Lemma 4 each sunny line can meet \\(T\\) in at most one point.  \nConsequently the two sunny lines \\(L_2,L_3\\) together can cover at most two points of \\(T\\).\n\nSince \\(|T|=3\\), at least one point of \\(T\\) would remain uncovered, contradicting the\nrequirement that every point of \\(S\\) lie on at least one of the three lines.\n\nHence no such configuration exists. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
