e n+1-r\\).  \n   Take the two extreme points \\((1,r)\\) and \\((n+1-r,r)\\).  \n   Their connecting line has slope  \n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{0}{(n+1-r)-1}=0,\n   \\]\n   which is forbidden. Choose instead the line \\(\\ell'\\) through \\((1,r)\\) and \\((2,r+1)\\).  \n   Its slope is\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{(r+1)-r}{2-1}=1\\neq0,-1,\\infty,\n   \\]\n   so \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.  \n   Because \\((2,r+1)\\in S_{n}\\) (indeed \\(2+(r+1)=r+3\\le n+1\\) for all admissible \\(r\\)), the line \\(\\ell'\\) contains at least two points of \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   Every point \\((a,r)\\) with \\(a\\ge3\\) also belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=r\\), which is still present in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (the original family contains all horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); see the construction in §4). Hence after removing \\(\\ell\\) and adding \\(\\ell'\\) all points of \\(S_{n}\\) remain covered.\n\n2. **Vertical**: \\(\\ell\\colon x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \n   The points of \\(S_{n}\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are \\((c,b)\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\).  \n   Choose the line \\(\\ell'\\) through \\((c,1)\\) and \\((1,1)\\).  \n   Its slope is\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{1-1}{c-1}=0,\n   \\]\n   which is forbidden. Instead take the line through \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c+1,2)\\).  \n   Its slope is\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{2-1}{(c+1)-c}=1,\n   \\]\n   a sunny slope.  \n   The point \\((c+1,2)\\) belongs to \\(S_{n}\\) because \\((c+1)+2=c+3\\le n+1\\) (the inequality holds for every admissible \\(c\\)).  \n   As before, any point \\((c,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which is present in the family described in §4. Hence the replacement preserves the covering.\n\n3. **Slope \\(-1\\)**: \\(\\ell\\colon x+y=s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\).  \n   Points of \\(S_{n}\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are \\((a,s-a)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le s-1\\).  \n   Take the two points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((2,s-2)\\).  \n   The line \\(\\ell'\\) through \\((2,s-2)\\) and \\((1,1)\\) has slope\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{(s-2)-1}{2-1}=s-3.\n   \\]\n   Since \\(s\\ge2\\), we have \\(m\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); thus \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.  \n   Both \\((2,s-2)\\) and \\((1,1)\\) belong to \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   The remaining points of the original \\(-1\\)‑slope line are either \\((1,s-1)\\) (covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is present in the family of §4) or have first coordinate at least \\(3\\); such points lie on the horizontal line \\(y=s-a\\), again present in the family of §4. Consequently the covering is unchanged after the replacement.\n\nIn each of the three cases we have exhibited a sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) that can replace the chosen non‑sunny line while preserving coverage of all points of \\(S_{n}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive construction of configurations with any prescribed \\(k\\)\n\nDefine \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) as in §1.  \nFor \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) perform the following inductive step:\n\n*Assume* we have already built a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBecause \\(|\\mathcal L_{k}|=n\\) and only \\(k\\) of them are sunny, at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) is non‑sunny.  \nApply the Lemma to that line; we obtain a new family \\(\\mathcal L_{k+1}\\) with \\(k+1\\) sunny lines and still covering \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nThe Lemma guarantees that the new line is distinct from all previously existing lines (its slope differs from the three forbidden slopes, while all previously existing non‑sunny lines have one of those slopes). Hence the families \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines for every \\(k\\).\n\nRepeating the step \\(k\\) times yields, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---------------------------------------------------