            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,\\ell_{i}:x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is vertical, therefore non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) covers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}= \\text{the line through }A_{i}=(i,n+1-i)\\text{ and }B_{i}=(i+1,1).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{i}\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nm_{i}= \\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=i-n .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have \\(m_{i}\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); hence every \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.\nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then \\(m_{i}\\neq m_{j}\\); therefore the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_{1}:x=1,\n\\]\n\nand for each integer \\(j\\) with \\(k+2\\le j\\le n\\) the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_{j}:x=j .\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n*The whole family.*  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\{V_{1},V_{k+2},V_{k+3},\\dots ,V_{n}\\}.\n\\]\nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*\n\n*   **Bottom row** \\(\\{(j,1)\\mid 2\\le j\\le k+1\\}\\).  \n    The point \\((i+1,1)=B_{i}\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\) for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).\n\n*   **Hypotenuse** \\(\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\).  \n    The point \\(A_{i}\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\).\n\n*   **Column \\(x=1\\).**  \n    Every point \\((1,b)\\in S_{n}\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(V_{1}\\).\n\n*   **Columns \\(x=j\\) with \\(j\\ge k+2\\).**  \n    For such a \\(j\\) the vertical line \\(V_{j}\\) contains all points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose abscissa equals \\(j\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(S_{n}\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).  \nConsequently, for each \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable family exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nNow we exhibit \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Sunny lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\).*  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) keep the lines defined in (2.1).  \nEach \\(L_{i}\\) contains the two points  \n\n\\[\nA_{i}=(i,n+1-i),\\qquad B_{i}=(i+1,1).\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n*The last sunny line.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{n}= \\text{the line through }(1,1)\\text{ and }(n,2).\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-1}{n-1}= \\frac{1}{n-1}\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); hence \\(L_{n}\\) is sunny and distinct from all \\(L_{i}\\) (\\(i\\le n-1\\)) because none of the latter passes through \\((1,1)\\).\n\n*Coverage proof