family exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Since the construction works for all \\(k\\) in the stated range and no other value is possible (the total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\)), the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Notation and elementary facts  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_{n}\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\,(n,1),\\,(1,n)\\).\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from  \n\n* \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n* \\(\\infty\\) (vertical),\n* \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nAll other slopes are allowed for sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe triangle \\(T_{3}\\) consists of the three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) we give three distinct lines covering these points and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n| \\(k\\) | family of three lines \\(\\mathcal L_{3,k}\\) | sunny lines |\n|------|--------------------------------------------|--------------|\n| 0 | \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;x+y=3,\\;y-1=2(x-2)\\) | the line \\(y-1=2(x-2)\\) (slope \\(2\\)) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\;y-2=2(x-1),\\;y-1=3(x-2)\\) | the last two lines (slopes \\(2\\) and \\(3\\)) |\n| 3 | \\(y-1=2(x-1),\\;y-2=3(x-1),\\;y-1=4(x-2)\\) | all three lines (slopes \\(2,3,4\\)) |\n\nIn each row every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the listed lines, and the number of sunny lines is exactly the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\nThus the statement of the problem holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Inductive construction\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have alread