e  \n\n\\[\nV_{n+1}:\\;x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(V_{n+1}\\) is not sunny (its slope is \\(\\infty\\)).  \nThe only line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) that contains the point \\((n,1)\\) is the vertical line \\(x=n\\); indeed every point of \\(T_{n}\\) with abscissa \\(n\\) has ordinate \\(1\\) because the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le 1\\).  \nConsequently we may **replace** the line \\(x=n\\) by \\(V_{n+1}\\) without losing any covered point:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\,n+1,\\,k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\setminus\\{x=n\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{x=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nAll points of \\(T_{n}\\) are still covered because the only point that could have been lost, \\((n,1)\\), now lies on the new line \\(x=n+1\\) after the index shift (the set \\(T_{n+1}\\) contains the point \\((n+1,1)\\) instead of \\((n,1)\\)).  \nAll points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) that are not in \\(T_{n}\\) have abscissa \\(n+1\\); they are covered by the newly added line \\(x=n+1\\).  \nThus every element of \\(T_{n+1}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n+1,\\,k}\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nWe have removed a non\u2011sunny line and added another non\u2011sunny line, so the number of sunny lines remains \\(k\\).\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n+1,\\,k}\\) satisfies \\((\\star)\\) for the pair \\((n+1,k)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one  \n\nAgain start from \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (for definiteness take \\(\\alpha=2\\)).  \nDefine the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_{n+1}:\\; y-1 = \\alpha\\bigl(x-(n+1)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(\\alpha\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore \\(S_{n+1}\\) is sunny and passes through the new diagonal point \\((n+1,1)\\).\n\nNow replace the vertical line \\(x=n\\) by the pair consisting of the vertical line \\(x=n+1\\) and the sunny line \\(S_{n+1}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal 