"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. **every** non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can occur.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n2. **Two extreme constructions.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on the unique line \\(x=a\\).  \n   *\\(k=n\\).*  Fix a real number \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha =2\\)).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y-1=\\alpha\\bigl(x-i\\bigr) .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,1)\\in T_{n}\\).  \n   Moreover, for any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b-1=\\alpha\\,(a-a)=0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every point of the bottom row \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) is covered, and the remaining points are covered as follows.\n   For a fixed \\(a\\) the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   b-1=\\alpha\\,(a-(a-b+1)) ,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. they lie on the line \\(L_{a-b+1}\\).  Consequently each point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\).  \n   All \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(\\alpha\\neq0,-1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \n\n3. **A flexible family of coverings.**  \n   For any integer \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\) we construct a family \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) consisting of exactly \\(m\\) *non\u2011sunny* lines (vertical, horizontal or diagonal) that already covers \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   The construction is elementary: choose an integer \\(p\\) with \\(0\\le p\\le m\\) and put  \n\n   * vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=p\\);  \n   * horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=m-p\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(p+(m-p)=m\\).  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have either \\(a\\le p\\) (hence the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a>p\\).  \n   In the latter case \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-p\\), and because \\(m-p\\ge n+1-p-(n+1-a)=a-p\\) we obtain \\(b\\le m-p\\); thus the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) indeed covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n4. **From a covering with \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines to a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\).  \n   *Step\u202f1.*  Use the family \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) from \u00a73; it consists of \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines and already covers \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   *Step\u202f2.*  Add \\(k\\) further lines, each of which is sunny and distinct from all lines already present.  \n   Because the covering property is already satisfied, adding extra lines cannot destroy it.  \n   The resulting set contains exactly \\(m+(k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n   The only point that requires justification is the existence of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that do not coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\).  \n   Choose a real number \\(\\beta\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and define, for \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\),\n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\;y-1=\\beta\\bigl(x-j\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(S_j\\) is sunny, and because its slope \\(\\beta\\) differs from the slopes of all lines in \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) (which are \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)), the lines \\(S_j\\) are distinct from every line of \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\).  \n   Moreover the \\(S_j\\) are pairwise distinct because they have different intercepts.  \n\n   Consequently the union \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) satisfies all requirements.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Two extremal families\n\n*All lines non\u2011sunny (\\(k=0\\)).*  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and for every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).*  \nFix a real number \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha=2\\)).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y-1=\\alpha\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,1)\\in T_{n}\\).  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  Write \\(c:=a-b+1\\) (so \\(1\\le c\\le n\\)).  Then\n\n\\[\nb-1=\\alpha\\bigl(a-c\\bigr)\n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\n(b-1)=\\alpha\\bigl(a-(a-b+1)\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equation of the line \\(L_{c}\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_{c}\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) cover the whole set \\(T_{n}\\).  \nBecause \\(\\alpha\\neq0,-1\\), each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering with an arbitrary number of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nLet \\(m\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\).  \nChoose an integer \\(p\\) with \\(0\\le p\\le m\\) and consider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{n,m}:=\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le p\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le m-p\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe family consists of \\(p\\) vertical lines and \\((m-p)\\) horizontal lines; therefore it contains exactly \\(m\\) lines, all of which are non\u2011sunny.\n\nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le p\\)*, then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(a>p\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-p .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m-p\\ge n+1-p-(n+1-a)=a-p\\), we have \\(b\\le m-p\\).  \nConsequently the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Adding the required number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k .\n\\]\n\nApply the construction of \u00a72 with this value of \\(m\\); we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) of exactly \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines that already covers \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nIt remains to add \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nChoose a real number \\(\\beta\\) with \\(\\beta\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (for instance \\(\\beta=2\\)).  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;y-1=\\beta\\bigl(x-j\\bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_j\\) is sunny because its slope \\(\\beta\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\).  \nThe lines \\(S_j\\) are pairwise distinct (different intercepts) and none of them coincides with a line of \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\), whose slopes are only \\(0\\) (horizontal) or \\(\\infty\\) (vertical).  \n\nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}:=\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\;\\cup\\;\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal C_{n,m}|+k = m+k = n\n\\]\n\npairwise distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny.\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) already covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\), adding the sunny lines does not destroy this property.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) satisfies both requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of larger values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe total number of lines in any admissible configuration is exactly \\(n\\); therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Thus no integer \\(k>n\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConversely, because there are only \\(n\\) lines, no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"

