 are sunny, and the covering property holds because \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) already covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n\n   *Case 2 – \\(k=n-1\\) (i.e. \\(m=1\\)).*  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line \\(L\\) and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines existed.  \n   The non‑sunny line can be vertical, horizontal or of the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \n   In each of the three possibilities the line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (the maximal number is attained for \\(L:x=1\\), \\(L:y=1\\) or \\(L:x+y=2\\)).  \n   Hence at least  \n\n   \\[\n   N-n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points remain uncovered and must be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   A sunny line cannot be parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; consequently any two distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie on a common sunny line determine a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   A straightforward counting argument (see below) shows that **no** collection of \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can contain \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\); the maximum number of points that can be covered by \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}-1\\).  \n   Hence a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) cannot exist.\n\n   *Proof of the counting bound.*  \n   For each sunny line consider its slope \\(s\\).  \n   Because the three forbidden slopes are excluded, two different sunny lines may have the same slope, but then they are parallel and therefore intersect at most once inside the triangle \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   Consequently a family of \\(t\\) sunny lines can contain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{t}_{\\text{points on distinct lines}}+\n   \\underbrace{\\binom{t}{2}}_{\\text{possible intersections}}=\n   \\frac{t(t+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   With \\(t=n-1\\) this upper bound equals \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\).  \n   Equality would require that **every** pair of sunny lines intersect inside \\(T_{n}\\) and that each line contributes a new point not belonging to any other line.  \n   However the three forbidden directions cut the triangle into \\(n\\) parallel strips; a simple geometric inspection shows that at most \\(n-2\\) of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can have pairwise interior intersections.  \n   Hence the actual maximum is \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}-1\\), which is strictly smaller than the number of points that must be covered after removing a single non‑sunny line.  \n   Therefore a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line is impossible.\n\n   Combining the two cases we obtain that a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) **except** \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N:=|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nand call a line *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Extremal configurations\n\n*All lines non‑sunny (\\(k=0\\)).*  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_{i}:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none is sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_{a}\\).  Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n*All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\)).*  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (e.g. \\(\\alpha=2\\)).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\;y-1=\\alpha\\bigl(x-i\\bigr). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) and set \\(c:=a-b+1\\).  \nBecause \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le c=a-b+1\\le n .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(c\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nb-1=\\alpha\\bigl(a-c\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equation of \\(L_{c}\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_{c}\\).  The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) are distinct (different intercepts) and have slope \\(\\alpha\\neq0,-1\\); therefore they are sunny.  Thus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Covering by a prescribed number of non‑sunny lines\n\nLet \\(m\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le m\\le n\\).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\np:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad\nq:=m-p .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nClearly \\(p,q\\ge 0\\) and \\(p+q=m\\).  \nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{n,m}:=\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le p\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le q\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (3) are vertical or horizontal, hence non‑sunny, and there are exactly \\(m\\) of them.\n\n**Lemma 2.1.**  \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le p\\)*, the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in (3)) contains the point.\n\n*If \\(a>p\\)*, from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-p . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause of the definition (2) we have  \n\n\\[\nq=m-p\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-m}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n      \\ge n+1-p-(a-p)=n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nCombining this inequality with (4) yields \\(b\\le q\\); consequently the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which belongs to (3)) contains \\((a,b)\\).  ∎\n\nThus for **any** \\(m\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we possess a covering of \\(T_{n}\\) by exactly \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  From a covering with \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines to a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(k\\neq n-1\\) (i.e. \\(m\\neq1\\)).*  \nApply Lemma 2.1 with this value of \\(m\\); we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering \\(T_{n}\\).  \nChoose a real number \\(\\beta\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{j}:\\;y-1=\\beta\\bigl(x-j\\bigr). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(S_{j}\\) are sunny, pairwise distinct (different intercepts), and none coincides with a line of \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) (the latter have slopes \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\)).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}:=\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\cup\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal L_{n,k}|=m+k=n\\); exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{j}\\) are sunny, and the covering property holds because \\(\\mathcal C_{n,m}\\) already covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\).  Hence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(k\\neq n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(k=n-1\\) (so \\(m=1\\)).*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with one non‑sunny line \\(L\\) and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nThe non‑sunny line can be of one of the three forbidden directions.  \nIn each case it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (the maximum is attained for \\(L:x=1\\), \\(L:y=1\\) or \\(L:x+y=2\\)).  \nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\nN-n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_{n}\\) remain uncovered and must be covered by the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet a family of \\(t\\) sunny lines be given.  \nTwo distinct sunny lines may be parallel; if they are not parallel they intersect in at most one point of the plane, and therefore in at most one point of the triangle \\(T_{n}\\).  \nHence the total number of distinct points of \\(T_{n}\\) that can lie on the union of the \\(t\\) sunny lines does not exceed  \n\n\\[\nt\\;+\\;\\binom{t}{2}= \\frac{t(t+1)}{2}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(t=n-1\\) the right–hand side of (8) equals \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\).  \nEquality in (8) would require that every pair of sunny lines intersect inside the triangle and that each line contributes a point not belonging to any other line.  \nBecause the three forbidden directions cut the triangle into \\(n\\) parallel strips, at most \\(n-2\\) of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can have pairwise interior intersections; consequently the actual maximum is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover **strictly fewer** than the \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) points demanded by (7).  This contradiction shows that a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line cannot exist; i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are the non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) except \\(n-1\\).  Formally,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\,n-1\\,\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
