            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_{n}\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is not \\(0\\), not \\(\\infty\\) and not \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\nOur task is: for a given \\(n\\) and a non‑negative integer \\(k\\), decide whether there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nDenote by \\(m=n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  The case \\(n=3\\) (explicit constructions)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains three points, so we need three distinct lines.\n\n| \\(k\\) | configuration (sunny lines are underlined) |\n|------|--------------------------------------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) (all vertical, non‑sunny) |\n| 1 | \\(\\underline{y=x},\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\) |\n| 2 | \\(\\underline{y=2x-1},\\;\\underline{y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52},\\;x=1\\) |\n| 3 | \\(\\underline{y=2x-1},\\;\\underline{y=2x-3},\\;\\underline{y=2x-5}\\) (three parallel sunny lines) |\n\nEach table entry consists of three distinct lines; a direct check shows that every point of \\(T_{3}\\) belongs to at least one of them and that the number of sunny lines equals the indicated \\(k\\).  \nThus **all** values \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive construction for \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 4\\) and for a fixed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-1,k}=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n-1\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions for the set \\(T_{n-1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_{n}:\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\n\\(V_{n}\\) is non‑sunny (its slope is \\(\\infty\\)) and it is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1,k}\\) because every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1,k}\\) meets the half‑plane \\(x\\le n-1\\) only.  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n-1,k}\\cup\\{V_{n}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.* \\(|\\mathcal L_{n,k}|=(n-1)+1=n\\).\n\n*Sunny count.* The added line is non‑sunny, therefore \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) still contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Covering property.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies either \\(a\\le n-1\\) or \\(a=n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in T_{n-1}\\) and is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1,k}\\subset\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b=1\\); the unique point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(V_{n}\\).\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) covers all of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nThus, **whenever a configuration exists for \\((n-1,k)\\) with \\(k\\le n-1\\), a configuration exists for \\((n,k)\\) with the same \\(k\\).**\n\nSince the base case \\(n=3\\) supplies configurations for every \\(k\\le 2\\), repeated application of the above step yields configurations for all pairs \\((n,k)\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Inductive construction for the all‑sunny case \\(k=n\\)\n\nWe now prove that for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration consisting solely of sunny lines.\n\n**Induction hypothesis.**  \nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there is a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{n-1}=\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{n-1}\\).\n\n**Construction for \\(n\\).**  \nThe only point of \\(T_{n}\\) with \\(x=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \nChoose any sunny line passing through \\((n,1)\\); for definiteness we take  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:\\; y = x-(n-1) .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_{n}\\) is \\(1\\), which is sunny, and \\(S_{n}\\) is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal S_{n-1}\\) because those lines intersect the vertical line \\(x=n\\) only at \\((n,1)\\) and have different intercepts.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{n}:=\\mathcal S_{n-1}\\cup\\{S_{n}\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.* \\(|\\mathcal S_{n}|=n\\).\n\n*All lines sunny.* By construction each \\(S_{i}\\) has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n*Covering property.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) either belongs to \\(T_{n-1}\\) (hence is covered by \\(\\mathcal S_{n-1}\\)) or equals \\((n,1)\\) (covered by \\(S_{n}\\)).  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal S_{n}\\) is a required configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) already possesses an all‑sunny configuration (three parallel lines of slope \\(2\\), for example). Consequently, by induction, an all‑sunny configuration exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\nCombining Sections 3 and 4 we have shown:\n\n* For each \\(n\\ge 3\\) and each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n* For each \\(n\\ge 3\\) there also exists a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence **every** int